{"id":"pay-steps-outpacing","title":"Job classes whose top pay rose far faster than the rest","question":"Which job classes saw their top hourly pay step rise at least ten points more than the typical class between fiscal 2014-15 and fiscal 2025-26?","finding":"Across 972 job classes the top hourly step rose 48.3% between fiscal 2014-15 and 2025-26 in the typical class, and nine classes in ten rose less than 55.7%. 72 classes rose at least ten points more than typical; 25 of them gained extra steps at the top of the scale and 47 rose that far on the same or fewer steps. The largest rise is Architectural Administrator (job class 5120): $57.01 to $109.12 an hour, up 91%.","how":"From the City’s table of hourly pay by job class and step, fiscal 2014-15 to 2025-26: for each class, the highest step in the latest schedule of the first year and of the last year. The \"typical\" rise is the middle value across all classes present in both years, which stands in for the general wage increases. A class is listed when its rise is at least ten percentage points above that.","limits":"The table lists classes paid on steps only, not managers paid on ranges, and says nothing of how many people hold each class. A change of class code or title breaks the comparison. The labour agreements and Civil Service Commission records would show why each class moved.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"job","t":"Job class, title as recorded","type":"text"},{"k":"code","t":"Class code","type":"text"},{"k":"then","t":"Top hourly step, fiscal 2014-15","type":"usd"},{"k":"now","t":"Top hourly step, fiscal 2025-26","type":"usd"},{"k":"rise","t":"Rise","type":"pct"},{"k":"typical","t":"Typical class","type":"pct"},{"k":"ahead","t":"Points ahead","type":"pct"},{"k":"stepsThen","t":"Steps then","type":"int"},{"k":"stepsNow","t":"Steps now","type":"int"},{"k":"note","t":"Note","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"job":"Architectural Administrator","code":"5120","then":57.01,"now":109.12,"rise":0.9141,"typical":0.48